From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: karsyboy <karsyboy@k-ops.cloud>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:41:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] granite: re-runnable Granite patching inside other work trees

checkout_granite.sh applied each Granite patch with git apply from the
PyroWave directory, accepting an already applied patch by its reverse
check. Two cases broke once the tree is vendored instead of a checkout
of its own:

- A re-run failed: the 203-nit patch edits lines the normalization patch
  added, so on a patched tree the first patch neither applies nor
  reverses.
- Inside another Git work tree (deps/PyroWave in a client checkout, a
  build directory in the host repository) git apply resolves the patch
  paths against that work tree's root and skips files outside the
  current directory.

Restore Granite's pinned files first, then apply every patch in
Granite's own checkout with -p2.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 checkout_granite.sh | 15 ++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/checkout_granite.sh b/checkout_granite.sh
index e4fbde8..37bbed8 100755
--- a/checkout_granite.sh
+++ b/checkout_granite.sh
@@ -29,12 +29,13 @@ update third_party/khronos/vulkan-headers
 # earlier ones): HDR-to-SDR normalization, 1:1 scaler overlays, then the
 # BT.2408 reference white. shaders/slangmosh_scaler.hpp is generated from the
 # patched scaler; the last patch changes only host code.
-cd ..
+#
+# Every run starts from the pinned files: a later patch edits lines an earlier
+# one added, so an already patched tree can neither take nor reverse them.
+# The patches are applied inside Granite's own checkout (-p2 strips
+# a/Granite/): run from this directory, git apply would resolve their paths
+# against any work tree this directory is nested in and skip the files.
+git checkout -- .
 for name in granite-sdr-normalization.patch granite-scaler-overlay.patch granite-sdr-reference-white-203.patch; do
-	patch_file="$PWD/patches/$name"
-	if git apply --check "$patch_file"; then
-		git apply "$patch_file"
-	else
-		git apply --reverse --check "$patch_file" || exit 1
-	fi
+	git apply -p2 "../patches/$name" || exit 1
 done
